<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yesügei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1171</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Yesugei Baghatur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yesügei was a chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a> clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> and the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Temüjin</a>, later Genghis Khan. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and was a sworn brother of the Keraite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ong-khan">Toghrul</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-history-of-the-mongols">Secret History of the Mongols</a>, he took his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoelun">Hö'elün</a> from a Merkit, and was poisoned by Tatars around 1171 while returning from arranging his son's betrothal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borte">Börte</a>.</p></div>
